dary endpoints to nearest edge (perpendicular to primary axis)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/19/code.py:33:#     same-color neighbor and that neighbor is perpendicular to the missing target side.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/19/code.py:389:                f\"yellow pivot {pivot} has non-perpendicular source {source_dir} and target {target_dir}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/19/code.py:462:                        assert perp, f\"boundary endpoint ({r},{c}) color {color} not perpendicular\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/15/code.py:61:  - The original `touches_edges()` logic is correct for one split or perpendicular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/4/code.py:51:#       encoded as two perpendicular one-cell-facing gaps.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/4/code.py:333:                assert perp, f\"logical boundary endpoint {(r, c)} color {color} not perpendicular\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/4/code.py:374:    Key insight: Background holes now split into two semantic classes. A hole claimed by one line is just a repaired gap and must be shown as gray, while a hole claimed by two perpendicular lines is a hidden crossing and must be shown in the winning line color. The same local reconstruction step reveals both.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/5/code.py:37:#     boundary endpoints, when they exist, still meet the edge perpendicularly.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/5/code.py:259:    # meet the edge perpendicularly, but interior endpoints are now allowed.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/5/code.py:275:                        assert perp, f\"boundary endpoint ({r},{c}) color {color} not perpendicular\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/28/code.py:327:                        assert perp, f\"boundary endpoint ({r},{c}) color {color} not perpendicular\"\n/home/lixinyu/sna