rsable and flanked by same-color bars perpendicular to direction.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/14/code.py:119:        assert len(options) <= 1, \"ambiguous turn: both perpendicular directions valid\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/14/code.py:149:        assert len(options) <= 1, \"ambiguous turn: both perpendicular directions valid\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/14/code.py:214:      a colored cell, turn 90 degrees into the unique perpendicular valid corridor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/2/code.py:12:      - Magenta triggers encountered on that line emit the perpendicular diagonal rays, and those rays keep going to the border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/2/code.py:16:      - Trigger rays still travel on the perpendicular diagonal family, but each ray now halts immediately before the first non-background obstacle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/2/code.py:24:      - If another blue segment occupies cells on the trigger's perpendicular slash-diagonal, the combo ray paints background cells until the first blue obstacle and then stops.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/2/code.py:45:    - The guaranteed blocking segment lies on one side of the emitter trigger's perpendicular diagonal, so it blocks a ray without sharing the trigger cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/2/code.py:250:    Reconstruct diagonal endpoint pairs as staircase connectors, then emit obstacle-sensitive perpendicular rays from any trigger the staircase touches.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/2/code.py:257:    - Whenever that staircase passes through a non-endpoint colored pixel, launch the two perpendicular diagonal rays in the trigger's color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/21897d95/D8/2/code.py:391:    \"\"\"Return the two bar directions perpendicular to *stem*.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/28/code.py:37:#      have exactly one perpendicular obstacle neighbor, which encodes the bend.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/12/code.py:142:        assert len(options) <= 1, \"ambiguous turn: both perpendicular directions valid\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/12/code.py:261:      perpendicular corridor cell. This handles straight, L, and Z paths using\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/16/code.py:20:#   of its perpendicular extension corridor: short -> red (2), medium -> yellow (4),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/16/code.py:181:    Connect the blue endpoint pairs with a periodic diagonal stripe, then convert on-path magenta seeds into length-coded emitters whose perpendicular beams become indexed magenta dashes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/16/code.py:190:    - Active trigger seeds on that path do not stay magenta: their output color is red, yellow, or green according to the total length of their perpendicular diagonal extension corridor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/16/code.py:191:    - From the first trigger on a segment, emit magenta dashes with gap 1; from the second trigger, gap 2; from the third, gap 3; continue analogously in both perpendicular diagonal directions until the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/9/code.py:110:    Complete same-color diagonal endpoint pairs, then let on-segment trigger pixels emit one finite perpendicular ray.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/9/code.py:117:    - Traverse that completed segment from top to bottom. Every non-blue pixel on the segment is a trigger. Its ray length equals its diagonal distance to the nearer blue endpoint, and its single perpendicular ray direction alternates by trigger order: 1st, 3rd, 5th... use the upward side; 2nd, 4th, 6th... use the downward side. Draw only that many cells and never overwrite non-background pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/9/code.py:120:    - A trigger's position on the blue carrier diagonal now encodes two outputs at once: closeness to an endpoint gives the finite ray length, while odd/even order among triggers on the same carrier chooses which one of the two perpendicular sides is painted.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/9/code.py:66:#    - The output adds a conspicuous second cue: the thicker perpendicular walls\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/10/code.py:23:#       1) a trigger is consumable, so after it launches the perpendicular rays\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/10/code.py:201:    - Whenever a connecting diagonal encounters a trigger pixel, use that pixel to launch two rays in the perpendicular diagonal direction until they leave the canvas, then erase the trigger itself back to background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/21897d95/D1/2/code.py:395:    \"\"\"Return the two bar directions perpendicular to *stem*.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:25:#      do magenta cells lying on it emit perpendicular diagonal rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:29:#      and the perpendicular rays begin beyond the two perpendicular X arms.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:33:#         3x3 X and emit the perpendicular rays from the X's perpendicular arms.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:40:#      The visible output is a blue main diagonal with a magenta perpendicular junction attached.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:59:#      perpendicular orientation can be assigned to them. The generator preserves this separation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:63:#      X arms that will later be repainted blue and for the two perpendicular arms that remain\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:65:#    - The perpendicular X arms and the post-X ray cells are reserved as output-only cells, so no\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:76:#      while magenta survives only on the perpendicular diagonal and the launched rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:95:#   - No planned ray or perpendicular X arm collides with any input pixel or earlier reserved stroke.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:271:    Expand on-path trigger pixels into diagonal X junctions and perpendicular rays first, then redraw the blue diagonals over them.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:279:    - From the two X arms that lie on the perpendicular diagonal, continue rays outward to the grid boundary, filling only background cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/21/code.py:283:    - The active magenta pixels are still detected by future blue-segment membership, but the construction order is reversed: magenta grows first and the blue backbone is the last paint layer, so only the perpendicular part of each X remains visible.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/19/code.py:26:    - Its emitted perpendicular rays are still magenta.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/19/code.py:167:    - If a completed diagonal contains magenta candidates, only the first one encountered from the upper endpoint toward the lower endpoint is active: it turns blue and emits magenta rays along the perpendicular diagonal to the border. Later magenta pixels stay magenta and emit nothing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:31:#      * D2/3: a trigger no longer emits both perpendicular rays; it emits only the branch\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:41:#        c>r, so it emits only the upward perpendicular branch and that branch is red.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:43:#        perpendicular branch and that branch is green.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:58:#    - Original `draw_segment()` assumed every trigger contributes both perpendicular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:137:    Return the only surviving perpendicular direction for an active trigger.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:139:    Above r=c means \"upward\" along the perpendicular diagonal; below r=c means \"downward\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:243:    Complete diagonal endpoint pairs, then let each trigger emit one ordered colored perpendicular ray.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:250:    - Walk each completed segment from its upper endpoint to its lower endpoint. The 1st active trigger on that segment emits a red perpendicular ray, the 2nd emits green, the 3rd emits yellow, then the cycle repeats.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/4/code.py:251:    - Each active trigger emits only one of the two perpendicular branches: cells with c>r use the upward branch, while cells with c<r use the downward branch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/1/code.py:12:#    perpendicular to the opening. This avoids trivial 1-pixel cases and keeps the visible beam\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/1/code.py:183:# - the source blob spans at least two positions perpendicular to the ray direction, so the count-coded\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/5/code.py:18:#     - Another non-background color emits perpendicular diagonal rays when activated.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/5/code.py:22:#     - D7/3 changes the trigger test from \"pixel lies on the path\" to \"pixel is one perpendicular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/5/code.py:45:#       invalid after the trigger moves off the path, so detection must scan perpendicular neighbors of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/5/code.py:53:#     - A trigger zone is the set of in-bounds perpendicular-diagonal neighbors of every interior path\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/5/code.py:223:    Reconstruct a hidden full diagonal between same-color endpoints, render it as a dashed line, then emit perpendicular rays from offset trigger pixels adjacent to that hidden path.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/5/code.py:226:    - The background is the dominant color. One non-background color appears as endpoint pairs on shared 45-degree diagonals, while trigger pixels of another color sit one perpendicular-diagonal step beside interior cells of those diagonals rather than directly on them.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/5/code.py:230:    - For every non-line pixel found one perpendicular-diagonal step away from any interior hidden-path cell, extend that pixel's color along the path's perpendicular diagonal in both directions, starting from the trigger pixel itself and filling only background cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/21897d95/D17/1/code.py:452:    \"\"\"Return the two bar directions perpendicular to *stem*.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/28/code.py:118:    Match multiple blue endpoints by nearest diagonal pairing, then let every magenta point on those matched segments emit all perpendicular rays implied by the segments that pass through it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/28/code.py:121:    - The dominant color is background. Blue pixels form an even-sized endpoint set, typically with two short pairs on one diagonal plus another pair on the perpendicular diagonal. Magenta pixels may sit on one matched segment or at an intersection shared by multiple matched segments.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/28/code.py:125:    - Whenever a non-blue pixel lies on a drawn segment, treat it as a trigger for that segment's perpendicular diagonal directions. If the same magenta pixel lies on several matched segments, union all contributed directions and extend magenta rays to the border, filling only background cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/28/code.py:128:    - The decisive reasoning step is global pairing, not extreme-to-extreme connection. Once the correct short segments are chosen, a shared trigger can inherit perpendicular directions from more than one segment, so the final magenta pattern reflects both the pairing choice and the segment interaction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/3/code.py:610:    - Then move each isolated pixel along a 45-degree diagonal: one axis matches the outward radiate direction, while the perpendicular axis points away from the opening midpoint. Whenever that diagonal ray meets the wall+fill sprite, it reflects by flipping the blocked axis and continues until it reaches the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/34/code.py:259:    - Any trigger touched by a segment or by a later ray emits a new ray pair on the perpendicular diagonal orientation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/13/code.py:17:  - Replace the old perpendicular-diagonal trigger branches with orthogonal branches:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/13/code.py:30:    - From (3,3), emit the two perpendicular diagonal rays until the border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/23/code.py:221:    Recover square corners from blue bars, let a third marker choose the active diagonal, then emit perpendicular rays from trigger pixels on that diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/23/code.py:228:    - Whenever that chosen diagonal passes through a non-blue, non-selector pixel, treat it as a trigger and extend rays of that trigger's color along the perpendicular diagonal directions until the grid boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/21897d95/D4/1/code.py:22:#       they are center + (-p) + each perpendicular direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ce