, continue rays outward to the grid boundary, filling only background cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D13/2/code.py:113:    # Each active trigger remembers which perpendicular diagonal directions should emit rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D13/2/code.py:151:    # Emit perpendicular diagonal rays beyond the X endpoints.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/1/code.py:3:# Change: 垂直对角延伸（perpendicular_extension）——从品红像素出发，沿原对角线旋转90度的两个方向延伸至网格边界 → 正交延伸（水平+垂直）\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/1/code.py:16:#     old: the diagonal connector hit a trigger cell and spawned two rays on the perpendicular diagonal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/1/code.py:32:#       - emit magenta on perpendicular diagonal: (3,5),(2,6)... and (5,3),(6,2)...\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/1/code.py:45:#     - trigger collection previously stored segment-orientation-specific perpendicular directions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:3:# Change: 垂直对角延伸（perpendicular_extension）——从品红像素出发，沿原对角线旋转90度的两个方向延伸至网格边界 → 单侧延伸（基于品红像素位置）\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:19:#      segment. Originally each trigger emitted two perpendicular diagonal rays. In this\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:23:#      perpendicular ray. If c<r (\"below\"), it emits the downward perpendicular ray.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:26:#      * Segment \"\\\" through (2,4) uses perpendicular \"/\" directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:38:#    - The original solver stored both perpendicular directions for every trigger.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:80:    Pick the only allowed perpendicular ray direction for a trigger on a completed segment.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:105:    Complete diagonal endpoint pairs, then let each trigger launch only one perpendicular diagonal ray chosen by its side of the grid main diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:112:    - Whenever the filled segment passes through a trigger pixel, extend that trigger color along exactly one perpendicular diagonal direction until the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:113:    - Triggers above r=c (c>r) use the upward perpendicular branch; triggers below r=c (c<r) use the downward perpendicular branch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/3/code.py:163:    # Each trigger collects the single perpendicular direction implied by every segment that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D3/2/code.py:14:#      new rule: width = blob span perpendicular to motion.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D3/2/code.py:139:    \"\"\"One source/hit cell per perpendicular lane, using the furthest cell in direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D3/2/code.py:464:    - The emitted width equals the blob's full span perpendicular to travel, so the cascade continues with shape-sized beams until no new blob is hit.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D2/2/code.py:3:# Change: 垂直对角延伸（perpendicular_extension）——从品红像素出发，沿原对角线旋转90度的两个方向延伸至网格边界 → 反射式延伸\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D6/1/code.py:126:    Connect the anchor tips of matching blue bars, then emit perpendicular rays from foreign-color pixels on those diagonals.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D6/1/code.py:133:    - Whenever that diagonal passes through a non-blue colored pixel, extend rays of that pixel's color along the perpendicular diagonal directions out to the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/0/code.py:31:    - Both (2,2) and (5,5) emit rays on the perpendicular diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/0/code.py:151:    - If the finished diagonal contains trigger pixels, only the first trigger encountered from the upper endpoint toward the lower endpoint sends perpendicular diagonal rays to the border; later triggers on that same diagonal stay inert.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D6/2/code.py:21:#      magenta cell, magenta rays are emitted along the perpendicular diagonal to the boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D6/2/code.py:159:    Read blue arrow sprites as diagonal launchers, then emit perpendicular rays from trigger pixels touched by those diagonals.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D6/2/code.py:168:    - Whenever that blue diagonal passes through a non-blue, non-background trigger pixel, preserve that trigger and launch rays of the same color on the perpendicular diagonal in both directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D12/0/code.py:325:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D12/0/code.py:335:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/2/code.py:21:#     - For each paired magenta pixel, emit perpendicular diagonal rays in both\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/2/code.py:30:#     - Original seed: all three magenta pixels fire infinite perpendicular rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/2/code.py:62:#       perpendicular ray bundles, while a lone distant magenta yields none.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/2/code.py:81:    Connect same-color diagonal endpoint pairs, then let paired trigger pixels emit bounded perpendicular rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/2/code.py:88:    - On that completed diagonal, repeatedly pair the closest adjacent non-line trigger pixels of the same color. Each matched trigger emits perpendicular diagonal rays in both directions, with length equal to the matched pair's spacing. Unmatched trigger pixels do not emit anything.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/2/code.py:210:_TRIGGER_COLOR = 6              # color for trigger pixels and perpendicular rays\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/2/code.py:232:    \"\"\"Compute all cells touched by bounded perpendicular rays from paired triggers.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D14/0/code.py:19:#       trigger pixels along the perpendicular diagonal direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D14/0/code.py:234:    - Every non-blue pixel encountered on such a diagonal emits magenta rays along the perpendicular diagonal directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D14/0/code.py:237:    Key insight: Triggers still generate perpendicular diagonal rays, but crossings are no longer governed by draw order; blue-line / magenta-ray overlaps become explicit red collision markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D12/2/code.py:274:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D12/2/code.py:284:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D1/0/code.py:25:#     - This variant keeps the \"connect endpoints, then trigger perpendicular rays\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D1/0/code.py:44:#       should be comparable: the main path is axis-aligned and the perpendicular effect\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D1/0/code.py:56:#     - Original perpendicular directions were diagonal `(dr,-dc)` / `(-dr,dc)`. For a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D1/0/code.py:57:#       horizontal segment the perpendicular directions are `(-1,0)` and `(1,0)`; for a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D1/0/code.py:89:    - Whenever that filled segment passes through a non-line pixel, keep that pixel and extend rays from it in the perpendicular orthogonal directions until the grid boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D1/0/code.py:213:    \"\"\"Compute all cells touched by perpendicular orthogonal rays from trigger positions.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D14/1/code.py:143:    Complete blue diagonals, then emit perpendicular trigger rays that stop short of blue collisions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D14/1/code.py:151:    - Each trigger sends rays along the perpendicular diagonal directions, but if a ray would enter a blue connector cell, the ray stops before it and that blue cell is also left blank.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/0/code.py:18:#       and emit perpendicular diagonal rays from differently-colored trigger\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/0/code.py:95:    - Whenever one of those diagonals passes through a trigger pixel, extend two rays in the perpendicular diagonal direction until they leave the canvas.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/0/code.py:214:    \"\"\"Compute all cells covered by perpendicular ray trajectories.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/1/code.py:307:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/1/code.py:317:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:17:#   happens after detection: instead of emitting both perpendicular-diagonal rays, each trigger now\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:21:#   5th... trigger fires in the perpendicular direction with row -1 (\"upward\"), while the 2nd,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:35:# - Original `draw_segment()` stored both perpendicular directions for every trigger. That depends\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:40:# - Original generator computed ray cells from a segment-wide pair of perpendicular directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:64:#   perpendicular rays. Only the count and side-selection of magenta rays changes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:76:    Complete same-color diagonal endpoint pairs, then let on-segment trigger pixels fire one alternating perpendicular ray.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:83:    - Read every completed segment from its upper endpoint to its lower endpoint. Non-line pixels encountered on that path act as triggers: the 1st, 3rd, 5th... trigger emits only the upward perpendicular diagonal ray, while the 2nd, 4th, 6th... trigger emits only the downward perpendicular diagonal ray, each continuing to the grid boundary without overwriting non-background cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:86:    - The magenta pixels are no longer symmetric splitters. They still activate only when the blue diagonal passes through them, but now their position order along that diagonal chooses which single perpendicular side is extended.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/1/code.py:17:#       magenta pixels lying on those segments trigger perpendicular diagonal rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/1/code.py:19:#       completion rule: each trigger emits both perpendicular rays for exactly as\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/1/code.py:29:#       each perpendicular direction: (1,3),(0,4),(-1,5),... and (3,1),(4,0),(5,-1),...\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/1/code.py:70:#       connectors, magenta marks triggers and the perpendicular completions. No\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/1/code.py:166:    - Whenever such a segment passes through a trigger pixel, emit the perpendicular diagonal rays in the trigger color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/1/code.py:167:    - Each perpendicular ray runs for as many steps as the blue segment length; if those completed rays leave the input rectangle, enlarge the output grid with background padding so the full result fits.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/3/code.py:314:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/3/code.py:324:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/18/code.py:150:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (col for vertical rays, row for horizontal rays).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/2/code.py:33:#       2. Emit the perpendicular slash ray through the trigger.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/2/code.py:37:#       2. Emit the same perpendicular slash ray, but skip the trigger cell itself because it is an original seed.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/2/code.py:72:    - Reconstruct each occupied diagonal segment by connecting the extreme endpoint pair, note every non-endpoint color encountered on that connector, and from each such trigger extend rays along the perpendicular diagonal directions until the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/2/code.py:76:    - The visible output is a filtered branch extraction: the sparse input seeds still describe the hidden connector geometry, but the answer keeps only the perpendicular ray pattern produced by those triggers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D10/2/code.py:182:_TRIGGER_COLOR = 6              # color for trigger pixels and perpendicular rays\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/2/code.py:366:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/2/code.py:376:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/25/code.py:171:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/25/code.py:181:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/0/code.py:17:      - a blue \"/\" segment is placed on the trigger's perpendicular diagonal, but only on one side of the trigger.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/0/code.py:19:      - the perpendicular ray paints background cells until the border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/0/code.py:29:    - The solver still uses the original \"find the line color, fill the extreme same-diagonal endpoints, then launch perpendicular rays\" structure.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/0/code.py:43:    - The guaranteed barrier segment lies on the trigger's perpendicular diagonal but excludes the trigger cell itself, so the ray collides with the barrier rather than with an invalid overlap.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/0/code.py:221:    Fill same-color diagonal endpoint pairs, then grow perpendicular trigger rays that stop at the first obstacle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/0/code.py:228:    - Whenever that completed diagonal passes through a trigger pixel, launch the two perpendicular diagonal rays in the trigger color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/1/code.py:12:  two perpendicular diagonal rays until the grid edge. In this variant, each ray length is the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/1/code.py:17:  blue endpoint, then draws exactly 3 cells in each perpendicular slash direction and stops even\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/1/code.py:22:  the trigger sits from the nearest blue endpoint, then expect the perpendicular magenta rays to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/1/code.py:42:- A trigger is legal only if both perpendicular rays of its fixed length stay in-bounds and there\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/1/code.py:81:    Connect same-color diagonal pixel pairs, then emit perpendicular finite rays from trigger pixels on those diagonals.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/1/code.py:88:    - Whenever that segment passes through a non-blue trigger pixel, emit the two perpendicular diagonal rays in the trigger color, but only for a fixed number of cells equal to the trigger's distance to the nearer blue endpoint on that segment.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/1/code.py:138:    # Each trigger stores the emitted perpendicular directions and the fixed length\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/0/code.py:76:    - When a filled diagonal passes through a magenta trigger pixel, launch magenta rays along the perpendicular diagonal in both directions until the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/0/code.py:178:    \"\"\"Compute all cells touched by perpendicular rays from trigger positions.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D15/1/code.py:111:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D15/1/code.py:121:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b80bb43/D1/3/code.py:69:    - Restore the broken line and add a perpendicular segment of the same length through the gap center.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/31/code.py:87:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/31/code.py:97:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/1/code.py:104:    Pair multiple blue diagonal endpoints by nearest matching, then emit perpendicular rays from trigger pixels on the chosen segments.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/1/code.py:111:    - Draw each matched segment in blue. Whenever a non-blue pixel lies on one of those segments, extend rays from it along the perpendicular diagonal directions until the grid border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:16:#     original: a trigger pixel on a completed blue diagonal emits two perpendicular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:18:#     variant: each perpendicular arm stops after a finite number of cells. That\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:25:#       is the endpoint-to-endpoint diagonal step count, then each perpendicular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:34:#       original: perpendicular slash-diagonal rays run from (3,3) all the way to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:58:#       in-bounds on both perpendicular directions because it mirrors the available\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:81:    Complete same-color diagonal endpoint pairs, then emit bounded perpendicular diagonals from trigger pixels on those segments.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:88:    - Whenever that filled diagonal passes through a trigger pixel, draw the two perpendicular diagonal arms in the trigger's color, but stop each arm after a number of steps equal to the trigger's shorter distance to the two segment endpoints.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:91:    - A trigger no longer shoots to the canvas edge; its perpendicular extension is measured by how centrally it sits on the completed diagonal, so middle triggers create longer crossbars than near-end triggers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/3/code.py:219:    \"\"\"Compute cells touched by finite perpendicular rays.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/2/code.py:22:#      diagonal still emit perpendicular magenta rays exactly as before.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/2/code.py:95:    - Whenever that segment passes through a magenta pixel, launch magenta rays along the perpendicular diagonal direction until the grid edge, filling only background cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D6/3/code.py:330:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D6/3/code.py:340:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/0/code.py:33:#       X corners -> emit perpendicular diagonal rays (original behavior)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/0/code.py:50:#     an X symbol, that trigger still emits the original perpendicular diagonal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/0/code.py:56:#   keep only three ray families: perpendicular diagonal, horizontal, vertical\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/0/code.py:91:    - Whenever the filled path passes through a magenta pixel, emit the perpendicular diagonal rays in magenta; whenever it passes through a red pixel, emit orthogonal horizontal and vertical rays in red.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/1/code.py:56:    Connect blue diagonal endpoint pairs, then emit capped perpendicular magenta rays from trigger pixels on those diagonals.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/1/code.py:63:    - If the segment passes through a trigger pixel, shoot perpendicular diagonal rays in the trigger's color, but cap each ray at floor(endpoint distance / 2) cells instead of drawing all the way to the border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/1/code.py:66:    - The blue endpoint spacing is a visible numeric code: longer blue gaps produce longer magenta extensions, while shorter blue gaps truncate them sooner along the perpendicular diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/1/code.py:189:    \"\"\"Cells painted by the capped perpendicular rays.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D14/1/code.py:360:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D14/1/code.py:370:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/1/code.py:19:#      1. domino blocks (area 2, either 1x2 or 2x1) -> perpendicular rays extend exactly 2 cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/1/code.py:20:#      2. 2x2 square blocks (area 4) -> perpendicular rays extend exactly 4 cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/1/code.py:25:#      perpendicular line.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/1/code.py:137:    Complete blue diagonals, then emit length-limited perpendicular stubs from magenta trigger blocks.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/1/code.py:145:    - For each activated magenta block, emit perpendicular diagonal rays from the block cells touched by the blue diagonal. Domino blocks extend 2 cells per direction; 2x2 square blocks extend 4 cells per direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/2/code.py:100:    Complete the longest-color diagonal segments, then let on-segment magenta pixels read local instruction markers to choose which perpendicular rays to emit.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/2/code.py:107:    - Whenever the filled diagonal passes through a magenta trigger, inspect that trigger's north/south neighbors in the original input: red above means emit only the upward perpendicular diagonal ray, green below means emit only the downward perpendicular diagonal ray, and having both markers means emit both rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D17/2/code.py:110:    - The magenta pixels are no longer self-sufficient beam splitters; their local red/green neighborhood encodes which subset of the two perpendicular branches should appear.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/3/code.py:16:#     - grid size, colors, diagonal endpoint pairing, and perpendicular-ray idea stay the same.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/3/code.py:20:#     - instead, a trigger sits one perpendicular-diagonal step away from an interior line cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/3/code.py:35:#     - adjacency is restricted to exactly one perpendicular-diagonal step. This is visibly different\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/3/code.py:194:    Fill same-color diagonal endpoint pairs, then emit perpendicular rays from nearby off-line trigger pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/3/code.py:197:    - The background is the dominant color. One non-background color appears as endpoint pairs on shared 45-degree diagonals, while trigger pixels of another color sit one perpendicular-diagonal step beside those segments instead of on them.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/3/code.py:201:    - For every non-line pixel adjacent to an interior segment cell by one perpendicular-diagonal step, extend that pixel's color along the segment's perpendicular diagonal in both directions, starting from the trigger pixel itself.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/3/code.py:204:    - The important interaction is no longer \"diagonal hits a colored cell\". The solver has to notice which nearby side pixel belongs to the diagonal, then use the diagonal's perpendicular orientation to launch a ray from that offset marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D12/0/code.py:105:    - For every same-color diagonal endpoint pair, fill both diagonals of its bounding square: the direct endpoint-to-endpoint diagonal and the perpendicular diagonal through the same midpoint.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D7/2/code.py:319:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D7/2/code.py:329:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/7/code.py:20:#      length. Instead, width equals the blob span perpendicular to travel, implemented as\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/7/code.py:139:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/7/code.py:149:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/7/code.py:483:    - The outgoing width is the blob's full span perpendicular to travel, so larger\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/2/code.py:17:#      The first magenta encountered on that ordered path emits the same perpendicular rays as before\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/2/code.py:63:    - If that trace reaches a differently-colored pixel, do not pass through it. Instead, keep that obstacle pixel as-is and emit two perpendicular diagonal rays in the obstacle's color out to the grid boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/2/code.py:66:    - Magenta is no longer a transparent trigger. It is a blocker-and-splitter: it marks where the blue diagonal terminates and where the perpendicular magenta rays begin.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D12/2/code.py:66:    Use blue corner pairs to identify squares, then let a third marker choose which diagonal is drawn before emitting perpendicular rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D12/2/code.py:73:    - Whenever that chosen diagonal passes through a non-blue, non-red pixel, treat it as a trigger and extend magenta rays along the perpendicular diagonal direction until the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D15/1/code.py:125:    Connect the diagonal endpoint pair, but only paint on the designated stripe background, and emit stripe-constrained perpendicular rays from trigger pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D15/1/code.py:132:    - When the connection hits a differently colored pixel, emit the usual perpendicular diagonal rays, but both segment fills and ray fills may only land on cells belonging to the active stripe background color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D3/1/code.py:210:      same-color pair on perpendicular edges.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/15/code.py:119:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/15/code.py:124:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/13/code.py:324:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/13/code.py:334:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D5/0/code.py:13:    - Segments stay on distinct parallel lines (distinct perpendicular offsets),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/14/code.py:133:    \"\"\"Sort coordinates by their perpendicular value relative to direction d.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/14/code.py:169:    - Any blob touched within budget is activated: split its blue body across the axis perpendicular to the port direction, color the port-facing half red, keep the far half blue, then emit a thick ray through the green port.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/14/code.py:336:    \"\"\"Coordinate perpendicular to direction d (column for vertical, row for horizontal).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D5/1/code.py:31:#    - The old reference logic depended on perpendicular-offset median and odd segment\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D5/1/code.py:39:#    - The generator still places one segment per perpendicular offset, keeps every cell in\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D5/1/code.py:44:#    - The reference segment is forced away from the median perpendicular offset, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D9/1/code.py:179:    - Inside each group, keep the original reference rule: order the parallel lines by perpendicular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D9/1/code.py:207:        assert len(s_vals) == len(set(s_vals)), \"perpendicular offsets inside a group must be unique\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D11/0/code.py:24:#    four directions, same odd number of parallel segments, same median perpendicular-offset reference, same\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D11/0/code.py:175:    - Use the segment at the median perpendicular offset as the reference.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/0/code.py:16:#    Input now differs from the original task in one visible way: the perpendicular offsets are not equally spaced.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/0/code.py:31:#      That assumes each segment keeps its old perpendicular offset. It is false in this variant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/0/code.py:46:#    Minimum perpendicular gaps remain >= 2 for orthogonal directions and >= 4 for diagonals, so parallel lines do\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/0/code.py:173:    - There are 3 or 5 multi-cell segments, all parallel and all lying on distinct perpendicular offsets.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/0/code.py:174:    - Their perpendicular gaps are intentionally uneven in the input, while their lengths and aligned endpoint may vary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/0/code.py:177:    - Detect the common direction and sort the segments by perpendicular offset.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D8/0/code.py:17:#    segment whose perpendicular offset is the median and uses that line as the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D8/0/code.py:46:#    - Segment extraction assumes each logical line has a unique perpendicular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D8/0/code.py:71:#    Only parallel line segments are present. Distinct perpendicular offsets and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D8/0/code.py:129:    - Detect the common direction, extract each multi-cell segment, and choose the segment at the median perpendicular offset as the reference.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/1/code.py:27:#      perpendicular line. Once lines are reassigned by rank, source identity and destination slot\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/1/code.py:49:#    - perpendicular offsets are unique and ordered, so slot positions are well-defined;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/1/code.py:122:    - Use the segment at the median perpendicular offset as the reference; its start position and length define the common output extent.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D6/1/code.py:123:    - Rank the input segments from longest to shortest, rank the slot positions by perpendicular offset, and place the i-th longest color onto the i-th slot.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D11/1/code.py:21:#    Input still contains 3 or 5 parallel same-direction lines on distinct perpendicular offsets,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D11/1/code.py:43:#    - \"one recovered segment -> one perpendicular offset\" used to hold; now a single logical line\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D11/1/code.py:75:#    - main() assumes the merged logical segments still have unique perpendicular offsets and odd\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e376de54/D11/1/code.py:208:    - Detect the dominant shared direction, trace each continuous run, and merge runs that share both color and perpendicular offset into one logical segment spanning across the gap.\n/home/lixin